    <!-- Comprehensive Guide to Shoe Sizing in CM (500+ words) -->
    <section class="content-band">
      <div class="page-inner">
        <article class="prose-card">
          <header class="prose-header">
            <p class="mono-eyebrow">Metric Footwear Guide</p>
            <h2 class="prose-main-title">Understanding Centimeter (CM) and Mondopoint Shoe Sizing</h2>
            <p class="prose-lead">Centimeter-based shoe sizing is the most transparent footwear standard in the world. Learn how Mondopoint works and why sneakerheads trust the CM tongue label.</p>
          </header>

          <div class="content-prose">
            <p>Centimeter-based shoe sizing is the most transparent footwear standard in the world. Officially formalized by the International Organization for Standardization as the Mondopoint system (ISO 9407), it specifies shoe sizes using actual foot dimensions rather than arbitrary numbers. While the United States and the United Kingdom still rely on centuries-old imperial barleycorn increments, countries like Japan, South Korea, and international ski boot manufacturers base shoe sizing directly on foot length in centimeters or millimeters.</p>

            <h3 class="heading-md">Why UK Shoe Sizes and Centimeters Don't Convert Cleanly</h3>
            <p>One of the most frequent sources of sizing confusion occurs when converting between the British (UK) system and centimeters. The UK shoe system steps by exactly <strong>one-third of an inch (approx. 8.46 mm)</strong> per full size, and <strong>one-sixth of an inch (approx. 4.23 mm)</strong> per half size. Because these imperial fractions do not align with clean decimal metric numbers, conversions always involve slight rounding:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>UK Men's 6:</strong> Fits a foot length of approximately 24.5 cm (9.6 inches).</li>
              <li><strong>UK Men's 7:</strong> Fits a foot length of approximately 25.5 cm (10.0 inches).</li>
              <li><strong>UK Men's 8:</strong> Fits a foot length of approximately 26.5 cm (10.4 inches).</li>
              <li><strong>UK Men's 9:</strong> Fits a foot length of approximately 27.5 cm (10.8 inches).</li>
            </ul>
            <p>If your foot measures exactly 27.0 cm, you fall directly between a UK 8 (26.5 cm) and a UK 8.5 (27.0 cm). In such cases, experienced shoe fitters recommend selecting the UK 8.5 to prevent toe pressure during walking.</p>

            <h3 class="heading-md">UK Child Shoe Sizes in CM: A Practical Parent's Guide</h3>
            <p>The UK children's shoe scale often puzzles parents because it resets. It starts with infant size 0 (approx. 9.5 cm) and runs through child size 13.5 (approx. 20.5 cm), after which adult sizing begins again at size 1 (approx. 21.0 cm). Here is how common UK child sizes correspond to foot length in centimeters:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>UK Child 3 to 4:</strong> Fits toddler feet measuring 11.5 to 12.5 cm (typically 12 to 18 months).</li>
              <li><strong>UK Child 6 to 7:</strong> Fits toddler feet measuring 14.2 to 15.0 cm (around 2 to 3 years old).</li>
              <li><strong>UK Child 9 to 10:</strong> Fits younger school children with feet measuring 16.7 to 17.5 cm (4 to 5 years old).</li>
              <li><strong>UK Child 12 to 13:</strong> Fits growing children with feet measuring 19.2 to 20.0 cm (6 to 7 years old).</li>
            </ul>
            <p>When shopping for growing kids, measure their feet every 8 to 12 weeks. Always choose a shoe size that provides 10 to 12 mm of clear space in front of the longest toe to accommodate active toe splay and monthly growth.</p>

            <h3 class="heading-md">How to Use the CM Tongue Tag When Buying Sneakers</h3>
            <p>If you collect or buy athletic sneakers from brands like Nike, Adidas, Asics, New Balance, or Puma, you may have noticed that their US-to-EU conversion charts often contradict each other. For example, a Nike US 9 is labeled as EU 42.5, whereas an Adidas US 9 is labeled as EU 42 2/3.</p>

            <div class="prose-callout">
              <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
              <p><strong>Sneakerhead Pro Tip:</strong> Ignore the US and EU size numbers when comparing brands. Look exclusively at the <strong>JP or CM measurement</strong> printed on the inner tongue label. A 27.0 CM shoe provides consistent length across almost every brand worldwide.</p>
            </div>

            <p>A 27.0 cm foot will fit comfortably in a 27.0 CM shoe across almost every athletic brand, regardless of how the manufacturer translates that size into US or European figures.</p>

            <h3 class="heading-md">Measuring Foot Width in Centimeters</h3>
            <p>Foot length is only half of the sizing equation. Foot width is measured across the first and fifth metatarsal joints (the ball of the foot). For an average adult foot measuring 26 cm in length, a standard medium width (D width for men, B width for women) measures between 9.5 and 10.0 cm flat across the ball. If your measurement exceeds 10.4 cm, you will likely find standard footwear tight across the sides, and should look for wide (2E) or extra-wide (4E) options rather than simply buying a longer shoe.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ Accordion -->
    <section class="faq-band" id="faq">
      <div class="page-inner">
        <p class="mono-eyebrow">FAQ</p>
        <h2 class="heading-md">Frequently Asked Questions: Shoe Size in CM</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          {faqs.map((f, i) => (
            <div class="faq-item">
              <button class="faq-trigger" aria-expanded="false" id={`cmfaq-${i}`} aria-controls={`cmfaq-c-${i}`}>
                <span>{f.q}</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">
                  <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true">
                    <line x1="12" y1="5" x2="12" y2="19"/>
                    <line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/>
                  </svg>
                </span>
              </button>
              <div class="faq-content" id={`cmfaq-c-${i}`} role="region" aria-labelledby={`cmfaq-${i}`}>
                <div class="faq-inner">{f.a}</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding: 48px 24px 40px; background: var(--v-canvas); }
  .page-inner { max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; }
  .hero-sub { margin: 12px 0 20px; max-width: 600px; }
  .hero-badges { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; }

  .quick-band { padding: 0 24px 48px; background: var(--v-canvas); }
  .cm-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; }
  .cm-stat-card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; }
  .cm-val { font-size: 20px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--v-ink); font-family: var(--font-display); }
  .cm-meta { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 6px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--v-body); }
  .cm-meta strong { color: var(--v-ink); }

  .table-band { padding: 64px 24px; background: var(--v-canvas); border-top: 1px solid var(--v-hairline); }

  .cta-box { margin-top: 48px; text-align: center; padding: 48px 32px; background: var(--v-elevated); border: 1px solid var(--v-hairline); border-radius: var(--v-r-lg); }
  .cta-box h2 { margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .cta-box p { margin: 0 0 20px; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .page-hero { padding: 32px 16px; }
    .quick-band { padding: 0 16px 36px; }
    .table-band { padding: 48px 16px; }
    .cm-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.faq-trigger').forEach(t => {
    t.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const exp = t.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
      t.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(!exp));
      t.nextElementSibling?.classList.toggle('open', !exp);
    });
  });
</script>
